    <!-- Guide (explanation only) -->
    <s:String x:Key="GuideTitle">Guide — special task functions</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideIntro">Two tools in the "New task" form: ❓ Guide (this, explains) and 🧩 Event generator (builds the trigger). Below: copyable examples and how the generator works.</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideShutdownHdr">1) Server restart / shutdown</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideShutdownDesc">To restart/shutdown the server via a task: put "shutdown" in Program and one of these in Arguments. Suggested cadence DAILY or ONCE. (Copy with the buttons.)</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideEventHdr">2) What an "on event" trigger is</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideEventDesc">Windows writes an event whenever a service changes state: ID 7036 of the "Service Control Manager". param1 = service name (e.g. "Shaiya Game Server"), param2 = state (LOCALIZED: on Italian Windows "in esecuzione"/"arrestato"). A task can fire on that event. Example XML filter:</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideGenHowHdr">3) How 🧩 Event generator works</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideGenStep1">• Step 1 — Search the service (e.g. type "ps_game") and pick it from the list. No EventID to know: it is always 7036.</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideGenStep2">• Step 2 — Choose WHEN to fire: "Any change" (start or stop), "Only when it STARTS" or "Only when it STOPS".</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideGenStep3">• Step 3 — "Generated filter" updates itself showing the trigger XML. Press "✓ Use in task".</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideGenStep4">• Step 4 — The form sets cadence to EVENT with channel and XPath prefilled. Fill Program/Arguments (the action) and create the task.</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideGenStateNote">Note: "started/stopped" use the REAL server state text (param2 is localized); the generator detects it from events. If not found, it falls back to "any change".</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideFindHdr">4) Find the EventID / service name by hand</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideFindDesc">The generator does all this already. To inspect events by hand, in PowerShell on the server:</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="LblState">State:</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="LblChannel">Channel (log):</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="LblEventDelay">Delay (min):</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="TipEventDelay">Wait N minutes after the event before running (useful: gives server/SQL time to start). 0 = immediately.</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="BtnUseInTask">✓ Use in task</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="GuideGenerated">Generated filter (preview):</s:String>
